# Cheaper line of sight (O218; server/README.md "Cheaper line of sight (O218)").
# Game::isSightClear -> Map::checkSightLine decides every ranged attack: each attacking monster asks once a second
# (Creature::onAttacking), Monster::canUseAttack asks for every target in a target search, spells and a player's throw
# ask too. It called Map::isTileClear for each tile of the line, and that walked the quad tree from the root (12 pointer
# hops, QTreeNode::getLeafStatic) per tile, although the 8 or so tiles of a line inside one 8x8 block share one leaf and
# one Floor.
# Now:
#   * src/chaossight.h (new, header only, no engine types; server/tools/chaossight_test.cpp and
#     server/tools/sight_bench.cpp build it alone): ChaosSight::Line, the same stepping (same float arithmetic, argument
#     swaps, loop bounds and early exit) with the Floor of the current 8x8 block kept in two words on the stack of one
#     call. Nothing is kept between calls (a tile or floor added later is always seen) and nothing is allocated.
#   * map.cpp: Map::checkSightLine builds a Line over Map::getFloorAt (patch 0072) and Tile::hasProperty(
#     CONST_PROP_BLOCKPROJECTILE). Map::isTileClear and Map::isSightClear are untouched.
# The answers are identical: the test compares a copy of the stock functions on many random maps, lines of all slopes
# and lengths, both directions, other floors, floors and leaves that do not exist, and the edges of the 16 bit
# coordinates.
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/chaossight.h
@@ -0,0 +1,118 @@
